# Singularity Flow — What It Is, and How Mature It Is

*An honest assessment, graded the way the product grades its own evidence:
claims below are labeled **measured** (verified against the codebase),
**self-demonstrated** (the product applied to itself), or **unvalidated**
(plausible, awaiting external contact). Verification boundary: the last
audited repository state; the codebase moves daily.*

## What it is

Singularity Flow is a Git-native lifecycle kernel for AI-assisted software
delivery. A deterministic, zero-dependency CLI owns lifecycle state as a
state machine on Git branches; models — any vendor's, or none — draft,
clarify, and narrate through typed contracts, but cannot approve, skip a
step, mutate state, or declare work done. Every artifact is hashed; every
approval binds a named human authority to exact bytes and lapses if they
change; every completed story mints a deterministic receipt; the entire
evidence chain verifies offline from a bare clone. The one-line version:
**prompts generate work — Singularity Flow proves what work happened, under
which rules, and who said yes.**

Within the firm it sits *beneath* our existing tooling rather than beside
it: Copilot and coding agents perform on it, our Jira intake and Teams
notifications plug into it, existing Git workflows run unchanged on top of
it, and audit or risk review requires nothing but a repository clone. It
was built inside our constraints — Artifactory-only distribution,
locked-down machines, no hosted services, no data egress — so adoption
requires no new infrastructure, procurement, or security exception.

## What exists today — measured

- **The kernel**: publication as a transaction (isolated commits,
  compare-and-swap, disk-fingerprint conflict detection, journaled
  exactly-once recovery); pinned per-story configuration with a monotone
  tighten-only policy fold; work intervals with checkpoints, cross-machine
  resume, and deterministic reconciliation; approvals with authority
  groups, exact-hash binding, and automatic staleness; policy waivers
  recorded as waivers, never as approvals; rail escalation with all work
  preserved.
- **Scale and verification**: on the order of 1,150+ deterministic tests
  across ~140 files; 460+ static validations across ~97 conversational
  skills under six machine-stamped behavioral contracts; a CI tripwire
  that runs the complete story lifecycle with model executables replaced
  by failing fakes — "works without AI" is a build gate, not a slogan.
- **Surfaces**: CLI, a substantial VS Code extension (inbox, journey,
  stories, traceability, analytics, ~50+ palette commands), and Copilot
  chat skills — all computing the same truth from the same branches; a
  reviewer's full onboarding is one clone.
- **Instruments**: exact token/cost telemetry (≈42-token fixed session
  cost, demand-paged skills, reference-passing previews); impact receipts
  and pre-registered studies with quality-gated labels, evidence grades,
  and privacy floors; model-independence enforcement with per-operation
  policy; an append-only evidence ledger verifiable offline.
- **Documentation**: a seven-document suite (deep dive, architecture,
  user, product-owner, perspectives, narrative, skills) plus packaged
  in-product help topics — all written against verified code.

## How mature — by dimension

| Dimension | Grade | Basis |
|---|---|---|
| Architecture | **High** | Eleven primitives, five invariants, held consistently across ~20 specs; every recent capability shipped as composition, not new machinery — the strongest available signal that the foundations are right |
| Correctness discipline | **High, and honestly earned** | The kernel survived an adversarial self-audit that found and fixed six real defects (including a lost-update CAS blindspot), with reproductions published in commit history — the audit trail contains evidence *against* earlier versions, which is precisely what makes the current version's guarantees credible |
| Feature completeness | **Broad core, sharp known edges** | The full lifecycle, measurement, and model-independence layers are implemented; a tracked defect list is open (world-model merge-base data loss and resolution staleness; narration-layer enforcement gaps) and the schema-migration framework — named prerequisite by multiple specs — remains the one critical structural gap |
| Self-application | **Self-demonstrated** | The platform is developed under its own governance: clause-bound specifications extracted into its own check gates, tests bound to requirement IDs — anyone can inspect a repository where the governance claim demonstrates itself |
| Enterprise posture | **Strong by construction** | Zero runtime dependencies, no hosted services, no telemetry egress, air-gap capable, credentials never touched, offline verification — constraint-native for regulated and locked-down environments |
| Operability | **Adequate for pilot scale** | Doctor/sync/recover cover the failure modes; retention/archival at multi-year scale is designed-for but unbuilt |
| Internal validation | **Unvalidated — the honest zero** | No user outside the build effort has yet run it. Every belief about the human experience — that ceremony feels proportional, that refusals read as help, that POs will love the inbox — is currently plausible and uncorroborated. The product's own evidence rules would refuse to render its adoption thesis |

## The gaps, plainly

A short, tracked list: the schema-migration framework (≈2 days, blocking
receipt-minting at scale); the world-model second-observer fixes; the
narration layer's enforcement pass; two small hardening items. None is
architectural; all are scheduled work. The only gap that matters
strategically is the last row of the table above — and it is fixed not by
engineering but by two names and ninety minutes with a real epic.

## Verdict

**Architecturally mature, operationally self-proven, unvalidated with
internal users.**
This is a system whose foundations have stopped moving — new capabilities
now arrive as compositions of eleven stable primitives — whose correctness
culture has already survived its own adversarial audit in public, and
whose distinguishing properties (repository-resident truth, hash-bound
authority, model-optionality, honest measurement) are structural rather
than aspirational. The recommended next step is a supervised two-week pilot: one team, one
real epic, one product owner and one developer, ordinary stories — with
the platform's own receipts measuring the result. That pilot is also the
only remaining source of the evidence this assessment lacks. Broader
rollout should follow pilot evidence, not precede it — not because the
kernel isn't trustworthy, but because the human experience is unmeasured,
and this platform, of all platforms, should refuse to claim what it has
not observed.

**The ask, concretely:** two named participants, ninety minutes to start,
two weeks of ordinary work. No infrastructure, no procurement, no security
review beyond reading the repository — the platform runs entirely on
approved tooling we already have.

The fair one-sentence summary: **an internal platform with the correctness
discipline of a mature system and the user-evidence of a prototype — and
honest enough to say so, because saying so is the product.**
